workedExamples.solution_text:
For $x<0$, the gradient decreases as you move left to right, so the curve is concave down. For $x>0$, the gradient increases, so the curve is concave up.
The bending changes at the origin.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=0.85cm,y=0.85cm,every node/.style={text=black,font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\draw[black,->] (-2.3,0)--(2.3,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[black,->] (0,-1.9)--(0,1.9) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[black,thick,<->,smooth,domain=-2:2,samples=101,variable=\x] plot ({\x},{0.2*\x*\x*\x});
\fill[black] (0,0) circle (1.3pt);
\node[below right] at (0,0) {$(0,0)$};
\node[align=center] at (-1.3,1.1) {concave down};
\node[align=center] at (1.3,-1.1) {concave up};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

